   TUT Wuri Handayani; RISTEK BRIN},
Abstract = {We wish to offer a survey of our published work on the Archaeoacoustics
   of the sixth century basilica of San Vitale, Ravenna, in the context of
   the contemporary pandemic known as the plague of Justinian. The ordered
   sequence of reverberant aural encounters at San Vitale is posited as a
   method of spiritual and physical cleansing. The original metrical verse
   in the atrium of San Vitale refers to the church as an arcem
   (stronghold) from the cuntagia mundi (contagions of the world), a
   sentiment contemporaneous with the continued effects of the plague. An
   allegorical route-way from Christ's nativity to resurrection signifies
   transformation. The resemblance of San Vitale's interior to
   Constantinople's mese processional urban route and the two-story
   colonnaded frontages enclosing the Forum of Constantine - an intriguing
   example of an indoor acoustic space designed as a compressed network of
   recognizable signifiers echoing a specific outdoor urban space
   elsewhere.},
