Organization = {Air \& Waste Management Assoc (A\&WMA) China Sect; Inst Earth Environm,
   Chinese Acad Sci (IEECAS); Desert Res Inst (DRI); U.S. Environm Protect
   Agcy (EPA), China Light \& Power (CLP); Natl Nat Sci Fdn China (NSFC)},
Abstract = {Measurement campaigns for airborne particles along a pedestrian route in
   the city center of Milan were performed by means of a portable
   instrument consisting of an optical particle counter and a global
   positioning system (GPS) signal receiver. Based on the size-resolved
   particle number concentration data and on proper density factors
   experimentally determined for Milan urban area, the mass concentrations
   were calculated in terms of particulate matter with aerodynamic
   diameters <= 10 mu m (PM10), <= 2.5 mu m (PM2.5), and <= 1 mu m (PM1).
   Besides directly measuring the personal exposure to PM throughout the
   route, the measurement campaigns pointed out small spatial and temporal
   variations of the concentration ranges in the different urban
   microenvironments visited along the route as well as very peculiar
   features of the particles levels in the underground subway. These
   findings suggested that the personal exposure of pedestrians in the city
   center could be estimated by simply taking into account the exposure at
   the open air and in the subway. The comparison between measured and
   calculated exposures according to the microenvironment-based estimation
   results in reasonable accordance, even though the estimations tend to
   slightly underestimate (12\%) the actual measured exposure.},
