   on Life in the Mediterranean Region, Thessaloniki, GREECE, OCT 08-12,
   2005},
Organization = {Mediterranean Sci Assoc Environm Protect},
Abstract = {Air pollution in a modern city has become a serious environmental
   problem, because of the combined effects of various pollutants on the
   physical and mental health of citizens, and, in general, the quality of
   urban life. Among the sources of pollution, the car traffic is of main
   importance, in particular, the presence alone of moving and stationary
   vehicles, their tailpipe gases and the associated traffic noise. The
   main emissions from spark ignition car engines has been re-evaluated
   seasonally for the period 2004-05, in the city of Thessaloniki, by close
   monitoring of the following tailpipe pollutants, both at idle and 2000
   rpm: carbon monoxide (CO), carbon dioxide (CO2), oxygen (O-2), and
   hydrocarbons (HC). The present paper reconfirms the presence of a small
   number of gross car polluters in the urban environment of Greek cities.
   In addition, a set of sensory observations, i.e. aural and nasal, has
   been acquired simultaneously from vehicles under test. Based on the
   latter, a novel emission control method for the detection of gross
   polluters is examined.},
